  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1221 PM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nGOES Water Vapor satellite trends and 12Z upper air analysis\nfeature an upper-level low centered over the Mid-Atlantic States\nwith an upper-level ridge located along the U.S. West Coast. The\nLower Mississippi Valley remains in between these two features\nwith northwest flow aloft present across the Mid-South. Late\nmorning temperatures were in the lower to middle 40s areawide, as\nclouds associated with the upper-level low have pushed mostly\neast into the Tennessee Valley.\n\nShort-term model soundings indicate the potential for the\nredevelopment of low level clouds this afternoon, mostly across\nareas near the Tennessee River and north of I-40. Otherwise,\nmostly sunny skies will prevail elsewhere with highs ranging from\nthe 40s near the Tennessee River to the lower to middle 50s\nsouthwest. Short wave ridging will continue to build across the\nLower Mississippi Valley into Saturday with temperatures\nmoderating back into the upper 50s to lower 60s on Saturday.\n\nShort-term model trends indicate a northern stream upper-level\ntrough will rotate through the Mississippi Valley late Saturday\nnight into Sunday. An associated surface low will develop over\nthe Southern Plains and move into the Middle Mississippi Valley,\nwith a trailing cold front moving through the region during the\nday on Sunday. Despite favorable lift and shear, overall\ninstability is expected to remain weak with REFS/NBM indicating a\nnarrow ribbon of surface-based instability remaining limited to\naround a couple hundred J/kg at best. REFS/LREF joint\nprobabilities of surface based CAPE values of at least 500 J/kg\nand 0-6 km shear of at least 30 kts remain generally less than\n10 percent during this period. Thus, confidence in strong to\nsevere thunderstorms on Sunday is low with low-topped convection\ngradually congealing into a line before it exits the forecast\narea during the day on Sunday. Rainfall is expected to be light\nwith this system with total amounts averaging below two tenths of\nan inch.\n\nLong-term deterministic and ensemble solutions show upper-level\nridging building back into the Lower Mississippi Valley next\nweek. This will result in moderating temperatures next week with\nhighs rising well above normal into the upper 60s to lower 70s\ntowards late next week. The next chance for rain may come towards\nthe end of the week as an upper-level low develops in the\nSouthwest U.S. and pushes east into the Central and Southern\nPlains towards the end of the period. Long-range deterministic\nsolutions differ on timing and placement of this next system with\nthe GFS trending further south and slower than the ECMWF and\nensemble solutions favoring a dampened out open wave trough.",
